==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|Zoroastrism}} {{qualifier|dated}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|Zoroastrian|-ism}}, influenced by Greek, Latin, Arabic and Syriac reports of {{m|en|Zoroaster||Zarathustra}} as the “lawgiver” of the Iranian peoples, as reviewed in {{w|Thomas Hyde|Thomas Hyde's}} ''Veterum [[Persis|Persarum]] et [[Parthians|Parthorum]] et [[Mede|Medorum]] Religionis Historia'', 1700.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˌzɒɹəʊˈæstɹɪəˌnɪzəm/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˌzoʊɹ.oʊˈæs.tɹi.əˌnɪz.əm/|/ˌzɔɹ.oʊˈæs.tɹi.əˌnɪz.əm/}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|common}} [[Mazdaism]], the surviving form of the indigenous (pre-Islamic) Iranian ethnic religion.
# {{lb|en|scholarly}} The historical (pre-Islamic) indigenous beliefs and practices of the Iranian peoples.

====Usage notes====
The term may be considered offensive in living Iranian usage.<ref>'''2010''', Van Christian A. Gorder, ''Christianity in Persia and the Status of Non-Muslims in Modern Iran'' {{ISBN|0739136119}}, pages 22 and 36: <br>Zoroaster (Persian, Zardosht) {{...}} is not to be considered the founder of the religion but only its apt promoter. Followers of his teachings find the term "Zoroastrian" offensive and sometimes call themselves modestly followers of "a good religion" (''veh-den'') or, more frequently, "worshippers of God" (''Yazdan Parast'').<br>{{...}}<br>The term “Zoroastrian” is offensive to them in the same way that the term “Mohammedan” is offensive to Muslims. Neither religion worships their founder. The term they use to describe themselves is “Yazdan Parsat.” {{...}} Because the term Zoroastrianism is offensive, some have called them Mazdakites [sic!] given the fact that their god is Ahura Mazda. Detractors also have called them fire worshippers, which is a term that they detest.</ref>

